PKCE = Proof Key for Code Exchange, pronounced “pixy.”
PKCE is an extension to the OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow that protects against an attacker stealing an authorization code and exchanging it for an access token.
PKCE ensures that the application that started the OAuth flow is the one that can complete it.

It is especially important for browser/mobile/public clients, and modern OAuth guidance recommends using it broadly.
First, what's the problem?
Remember normal Authorization Code Flow:
Client
   │
   │ Redirect user
   ▼
Authorization Server
   │
   │ User login + consent
   ▼
Authorization Code
   │
   ▼
Client
   │
   │ Exchange code
   ▼
Authorization Server
   │
   ▼
Access Token

Suppose authorization code is:
code = ABC123

If an attacker manages to steal/intercept that code, we don't want them to be able to do:
Attacker
   │
   │ ABC123
   ▼
Authorization Server
   │
   ▼
Access Token ❌

PKCE adds an extra proof.
How PKCE works
There are two important terms:
Code Verifier
code_verifier

and
Code Challenge
code_challenge

The client first generates a random secret value:
code_verifier = random_long_string

For example, conceptually:
code_verifier = "X7a91KpQ..."

This stays with the client.
Then it hashes the verifier, normally using SHA-256:
SHA256(code_verifier)
          ↓
    code_challenge

More precisely, the challenge is typically the Base64URL-encoded SHA-256 result.
So:
Code Verifier
     │
     │ SHA-256 + Base64URL
     ▼
Code Challenge

Step 1 — Client starts authorization
Client sends the code challenge to the authorization server:
Client
  │
  │ code_challenge = XYZ
  ▼
Authorization Server

Notice:
code_verifier ❌ NOT sent yet

code_challenge ✅ sent

The authorization server associates that challenge with this authorization request.
Step 2 — Login and consent
Same OAuth flow:
User
 ↓
Authorization Server
 ↓
Login
 ↓
Consent

Then the server returns:
Authorization Code

to the client.
Authorization Server
       │
       │ code = ABC123
       ▼
     Client

Step 3 — Client exchanges the code
Now the important part.
The client sends:
Authorization Code
+
Code Verifier

to the token endpoint:
Client
   │
   │ code = ABC123
   │ code_verifier = X7a91KpQ...
   ▼
Authorization Server

The authorization server calculates:
SHA256(received code_verifier)
           ↓
   calculated challenge

and compares it with the challenge stored earlier:
Calculated Challenge
        ==
Original Code Challenge?

If they match:
YES ✅
 ↓
Access Token

If they don't:
NO ❌
 ↓
Reject request

Complete PKCE flow
This is the diagram you should remember:
CLIENT                               AUTH SERVER

Generate:
code_verifier
     │
     ↓
SHA256 + Base64URL
     │
     ↓
code_challenge


Client ─── code_challenge ─────────────►

                Login + Consent

Client ◄── Authorization Code ──────────


Client ─── Authorization Code ─────────►
       └── code_verifier ──────────────►

                                      │
                              Hash verifier
                                      │
                                      ▼
                              Compare challenge

                                    MATCH ✅
                                      │
                                      ▼
Client ◄────── Access Token ───────────

What if an attacker steals the code?
Suppose:
Authorization Code = ABC123

gets stolen.
Attacker tries:
Attacker
   │
   │ code = ABC123
   ▼
Authorization Server

Server asks for the matching:
code_verifier

But attacker doesn't have it.
Attacker:

Authorization Code ✅
Code Verifier      ❌

Therefore:
Cannot prove possession
        ↓
No Access Token ❌

That's the main purpose of PKCE.
Why not just use client_secret?
A backend/confidential application can securely store:
client_secret

But consider a mobile app or browser-based app.
The application is distributed to the user's device/browser:
Mobile App
Browser App
Desktop App

You cannot safely embed one permanent secret and assume nobody can extract it.
So:
Backend Server
→ Can securely keep client_secret

Mobile/Browser App
→ Cannot reliably protect a static client_secret

PKCE solves this using a new random verifier for each authorization attempt rather than relying on an embedded static secret.
Client Secret vs PKCE
Client Secret	PKCE
Static credential	Per-authorization random verifier
Stored by confidential client	Generated dynamically
Good for secure backend clients	Essential for public clients
Proves client authentication	Binds authorization request to token exchange
Could be dangerous if embedded in frontend/mobile app	No permanent secret needs to be embedded


They are not necessarily alternatives. Confidential clients can use client authentication and PKCE.
Normal OAuth vs OAuth + PKCE
Normal authorization code flow:
Authorization Code
       ↓
Access Token

PKCE:
Authorization Code
       +
Code Verifier
       ↓
Server verifies against
earlier Code Challenge
       ↓
Access Token

Interview-ready definition
PKCE is an OAuth extension that binds the authorization request to the token exchange using a dynamically generated code verifier and code challenge, preventing a stolen authorization code from being exchanged by an attacker.

For HLD/interviews, remember these four words:
Code Verifier
     ↓
Code Challenge
     ↓
Authorization Code
     ↓
Access Token

And the central idea:
Even if you steal my authorization code, you still can't exchange it because you don't have my code verifier.